// Question bank. Option o[0] is always the correct answer; options are shuffled at runtime.
const BANK=[{"s":1,"t":"1.1","q":"Your agent loop exits whenever the response contains a text block. Customers report the agent says \"Let me pull up that order for you\" and then the conversation just ends. What is the correct fix?","o":["Keep looping while `stop_reason` is \"tool_use\" and end the loop only when `stop_reason` is \"end_turn\".","Instruct the agent in the system prompt never to emit text in the same response as a tool call.","Raise the maximum iteration count so the loop has more room to finish before it stops.","End the loop only when the assistant text contains a closing phrase such as \"anything else I can help with\"."],"e":"Claude can return text alongside `tool_use` blocks, so the presence of text says nothing about completion. `stop_reason` is the authoritative signal: \"tool_use\" means execute the tools and continue, \"end_turn\" means finish. Prompt rules about text, iteration caps, and phrase matching are all anti-patterns for loop control."},{"s":1,"t":"1.1","q":"After the agent calls `lookup_order`, its next response asks the customer for the order number again, even though the tool ran successfully. Your loop executes the tool, then immediately re-sends the original messages. What is the most likely cause?","o":["The tool result is never appended to the conversation history, so the model has nothing new to reason about.","The `lookup_order` description is too short, so the model does not trust or use the result it receives back from the tool.","`tool_choice` is set to \"auto\", which lets the model ignore any tool results it decides are irrelevant to the request.","The context window is full, so the API silently truncates the most recent tool result before the model can read it."],"e":"The Messages API is stateless. Each iteration must send the full history including a `tool_result` block for every tool call. If your code runs the tool but never appends the result, the model reasons from the same context as before and repeats itself."},{"s":1,"t":"1.5","q":"Policy says refunds above $500 require human approval. Despite a clear system prompt rule, logs show the agent occasionally calls `process_refund` for amounts like $650. What change gives you a guaranteed fix?","o":["Add a hook that intercepts `process_refund` calls over $500, blocks them, and redirects to `escalate_to_human`.","Add a PostToolUse hook that detects completed refunds over $500 and reverses them before the model ever sees the result.","Rewrite the system prompt rule in capital letters and repeat it at the end of the prompt so the model weighs it more heavily.","Add few-shot examples showing the agent escalating several refunds of $600 to $1,000 instead of processing them directly."],"e":"When a business rule needs guaranteed compliance, use a hook that intercepts the tool call before it executes (a PreToolUse-style interception). Prompt wording and few-shot examples are probabilistic. A PostToolUse hook runs after the refund has already been issued, so it cannot prevent the violation."},{"s":1,"t":"1.5","q":"`get_customer` returns dates as Unix timestamps, `lookup_order` returns ISO 8601 strings, and order status comes back as numeric codes (e.g. `3`). The agent sometimes misreads which order is newer and what status means. What is the best fix?","o":["Add a PostToolUse hook that converts timestamps and status codes to one consistent format before the model sees them.","Add a PreToolUse hook that rewrites each outgoing tool call so every backend returns its data in the same consistent format.","Add a conversion table to the system prompt that explains both timestamp formats and the meaning of every numeric status code.","Ask the model to normalize all dates and statuses itself in a scratch step before it writes each answer to the customer."],"e":"PostToolUse hooks intercept tool results after execution and before the model sees them, which is exactly where data normalization belongs. PreToolUse acts on the outgoing call, not the returned data. Prompt-based or model-side conversion leaves the interpretation burden on the model at every turn."},{"s":1,"t":"1.4","q":"Human agents complain that escalated cases arrive with only \"Customer needs help with a refund\" and they must re-ask the customer everything. They cannot see the AI conversation. What should the escalation include?","o":["A structured summary: customer ID, what was asked and tried, root cause, refund amount, and recommended action.","The last five conversation turns pasted verbatim, so the human agent can read the most recent context in the customer's own words.","A short note to the customer asking them to restate their issue to the human agent once they are connected to the queue.","A sentiment score and the agent's own confidence rating, so the human knows how carefully the case needs to be handled."],"e":"Because the human lacks the transcript, the handoff must be self-contained: customer ID, a summary of the request and attempts, root cause, specific amounts, and a recommended action. A slice of raw turns can miss critical facts, and sentiment/confidence scores do not help the human act."},{"s":1,"t":"1.4","q":"A customer writes: \"Order #4471 arrived broken, I was charged twice this month, and I need to change my shipping address.\" How should the agent handle this?","o":["Split it into three issues, investigate each with shared context, then send one unified resolution covering all three.","Resolve the damaged order first, then ask the customer to open a separate conversation for each of the two remaining issues.","Escalate to a human immediately, because requests that contain more than one issue are too complex for autonomous handling.","Focus on the double charge because it has the largest financial impact, and tell the customer the others can be handled later."],"e":"Multi-concern requests should be decomposed into distinct items, investigated (in parallel where possible) with shared context, and synthesized into a single resolution. Multiple issues alone are not an escalation trigger."},{"s":1,"t":"5.2","q":"A customer's first message is: \"I want to talk to a real person. Now.\" A quick check suggests it is a simple tracking question the agent could answer in seconds. What should the agent do?","o":["Escalate to a human right away, without looking up the order or trying to resolve it first.","Look up the tracking information first, then offer the human handoff if the answer does not satisfy them.","Acknowledge the request, offer to resolve the issue, and escalate only if the customer asks again.","Run a sentiment check and escalate only if frustration is above the configured threshold."],"e":"An explicit request for a human is honored immediately, with no investigation first. The \"acknowledge and offer to resolve, escalate if they reiterate\" pattern applies to frustrated customers who have not asked for a human."},{"s":1,"t":"5.2","q":"A customer types in all caps that their delivery is three days late and this is \"ridiculous.\" They have not asked for a human. Reshipping is fully within the agent's authority. What is the best response?","o":["Acknowledge the frustration and offer the reshipment; escalate only if the customer then asks for a human.","Escalate right away, because strong negative sentiment is a reliable signal that the case needs human handling.","Skip any acknowledgement and process the reshipment, since emotional language is irrelevant to fixing the problem.","Ask the customer to rate their frustration from 1 to 10 and escalate the conversation if they answer 7 or higher."],"e":"Sentiment does not correlate with case complexity. When the issue is straightforward and within the agent's capability, acknowledge the frustration and offer the resolution. Escalate only if the customer reiterates a preference for a human."},{"s":1,"t":"5.2","q":"A customer asks the agent to match a lower price they found on a competitor's site. Your refund policy only covers price adjustments when your own site lowers its price. What should the agent do?","o":["Escalate, because the policy is silent on competitor price matching and the request needs a human decision.","Decline the request, because anything not explicitly allowed by the policy should be treated as forbidden.","Approve the adjustment, since a competitor price match is close enough to an own-site price adjustment.","Ask for a screenshot of the competitor price and approve the adjustment if it can be verified."],"e":"Policy gaps or exceptions, where policy does not address the specific request, are a valid escalation trigger. The agent should not invent policy in either direction."},{"s":1,"t":"5.2","q":"`get_customer` searching for \"Maria Garcia\" returns three different accounts. What should the agent do next?","o":["Ask the customer for an additional identifier, such as email address or an order number, to find the correct account.","Choose the account with the most recent activity, since that is most likely the person currently contacting support.","Choose the account that has an open order, because the customer is most likely writing about an active purchase.","Escalate to a human, because ambiguous account matches cannot be handled by an automated agent at all."],"e":"Multiple matches require clarification via additional identifiers. Selecting by heuristic risks acting on, or disclosing, the wrong person's account."},{"s":1,"t":"5.1","q":"Long support sessions are summarized every 20 turns to save tokens. Customers complain the agent later \"forgets\" that it promised a $247.83 refund on order #8891 by Friday. What is the best fix?","o":["Keep a persistent case facts block (amounts, dates, order numbers, commitments) in every prompt, outside the summarized history.","Summarize less often, for example every 40 turns instead of 20, so fewer specific details are lost during each compression step.","Move the agent to a model with a larger context window so that summarization can be avoided entirely, even in very long sessions.","Add a system prompt instruction telling the model to carefully preserve all numbers, dates, and amounts whenever it summarizes."],"e":"Progressive summarization reliably compresses away exact amounts, dates, and commitments. The fix is structural: extract transactional facts into a persistent block that never goes through summarization."},{"s":1,"t":"5.1","q":"Each `lookup_order` call returns more than 40 fields (warehouse codes, audit timestamps, carrier IDs), but returns handling only needs about five. Multi-issue sessions run out of context. What is the best approach?","o":["Trim tool results to the relevant fields, for example in a PostToolUse hook, before they enter the conversation.","Remove older tool results from the conversation history once the agent has already replied to the customer about them.","Tell the agent in the system prompt to ignore any order fields that are not relevant to handling a return request.","Switch to a larger context window so that the full 40-field results can stay in context for every turn of the session."],"e":"Verbose tool output stays in context for every subsequent turn. Trimming to relevant fields before results enter history fixes the cost at its source. Removing history breaks coherence, and a prompt instruction does not reduce tokens."},{"s":1,"t":"2.2","q":"`process_refund` returns the text \"Operation failed\" both for timeouts and for refunds outside the 30-day window. The agent keeps retrying expired-window refunds. What change best enables correct recovery?","o":["Return structured errors with a category, an `isRetryable` flag, and a description, marking policy violations as not retryable.","Limit the agent to three retries on any failed tool call before it gives up, apologizes, and escalates the case to a human.","Add a system prompt instruction that any refund which fails should be retried exactly once and then escalated to a human.","Include the HTTP status code in the error text so the model can work out for itself which failures are worth retrying."],"e":"Uniform errors prevent the agent from choosing the right recovery. Structured metadata (category such as transient vs business, `isRetryable`, description) tells it a timeout can be retried while a policy violation needs a different path and an explanation to the customer."},{"s":1,"t":"2.2","q":"During a database outage, `lookup_order` returns an empty list, exactly as it does when no order exists. The agent tells customers \"I can't find any order under your account.\" What should change?","o":["Return access failures as errors (with `isError` and retryable metadata) so they differ from a query that found nothing.","Have the agent retry every empty result two or three times before it tells the customer that no matching order was found.","Make the agent ask the customer to double-check their order number every time a lookup comes back with an empty result.","Have the tool return a placeholder order during outages so the conversation can continue without visible interruption."],"e":"An access failure (the query did not run) and a valid empty result (the query ran and found nothing) need different handling. Retrying every empty result wastes calls on true negatives, and placeholder data is fabrication."},{"s":1,"t":"2.3","q":"The product team wants to add 14 more tools (loyalty points, inventory, shipping labels, and others) to the support agent, bringing it to 18. What is the main concern and the better design?","o":["Selection reliability drops as tool count grows; give each agent only the tools its role needs and use scoped subagents.","There is no real concern as long as every tool has a detailed description, because good descriptions remove all selection ambiguity.","The concern is latency; host the 14 extra tools on a second MCP server so they load in parallel with the original four tools.","The concern is tool skipping; set `tool_choice` to \"any\" so the agent is always required to call one of the 18 available tools."],"e":"Giving one agent many tools (18 rather than 4-5) increases decision complexity and degrades selection. Scope tools by role. Moving tools to another server does not change how many the agent chooses between."},{"s":1,"t":"2.1","q":"You rewrote the descriptions of `get_customer` and `lookup_order`, but the agent still calls `get_customer` for questions like \"where is order #5521?\" The system prompt says: \"Always check the customer's details before doing anything.\" What is the most likely issue?","o":["The keyword-heavy system prompt instruction creates an association that overrides the improved descriptions.","The tool descriptions are still too short and need five to eight usage examples each before the changes take effect.","`tool_choice` must be set to force `lookup_order` whenever the customer's message contains an order number.","The model caches its tool selection behavior and needs a brand-new session before the new descriptions apply."],"e":"System prompt wording can create unintended tool associations that override good descriptions. After updating descriptions, review the system prompt for keyword-sensitive instructions that conflict with them."},{"s":2,"t":"3.1","q":"A new developer joins and Claude ignores the team's naming conventions in their sessions. Everyone else gets the conventions. A senior engineer says, \"I put them in my CLAUDE.md months ago.\" What is the most likely cause?","o":["They are in the senior engineer's `~/.claude/CLAUDE.md`, which is user-level and not shared via version control.","The new developer's Claude Code installation needs to rebuild its memory index before any project files can load.","The conventions are in `CLAUDE.local.md`, which only starts loading after the developer has run `/memory` at least once.","The new developer needs to start Claude Code with `--append-system-prompt` before shared project files are picked up."],"e":"User-level `~/.claude/CLAUDE.md` applies only to that user. Team conventions belong in the project-level CLAUDE.md (root or `.claude/CLAUDE.md`), which is committed and shared."},{"s":2,"t":"3.1","q":"Two developers on the same repository report that Claude follows the testing standards consistently for one of them but not the other. What is the best first diagnostic step?","o":["Run `/memory` in each developer's session to see which memory files are actually loaded.","Reinstall Claude Code on the affected machine to clear any cached configuration files.","Rewrite the testing standards with stronger wording such as MUST and NEVER throughout.","Move the testing standards into the system prompt using `--system-prompt` for both developers."],"e":"`/memory` shows which memory files a session has loaded, which is the direct way to diagnose inconsistent behavior across sessions or developers."},{"s":2,"t":"3.1","q":"Your monorepo has `packages/api`, `packages/web`, and `packages/billing`. Shared standards live in `standards/` (rest-api.md, react.md, pci.md, logging.md). Each package needs a different subset. What is the most maintainable setup?","o":["In each package's CLAUDE.md, import only the relevant standards files using `@path` references.","Copy the full text of every standards file into the root CLAUDE.md so all packages get everything.","Turn each standards file into a slash command that developers run before working in a package.","Paste the relevant standards text directly into each package's CLAUDE.md and keep the copies in sync."],"e":"`@` imports keep CLAUDE.md modular: each package includes only the standards that apply, with no duplicated text to keep in sync."},{"s":2,"t":"3.1","q":"The root CLAUDE.md has grown to 900 lines covering testing, API conventions, and deployment. It is hard to maintain and reviewers keep missing conflicting edits. What does the exam guide recommend?","o":["Split it into focused topic files in `.claude/rules/`, such as testing.md, api-conventions.md, and deployment.md.","Move the content into `~/.claude/CLAUDE.md` so each developer can maintain and review their own copy of the file.","Compress the file by removing all examples and keeping only a one-line rule for each convention it currently covers.","Convert each section into a skill so the content only loads when a developer explicitly invokes it by its name."],"e":"`.claude/rules/` holds topic-specific rule files as an alternative to one monolithic CLAUDE.md. Universal standards should stay always-loaded, so converting them to on-demand skills would change behavior."},{"s":2,"t":"3.3","q":"Terraform files (`*.tf`) live in `infra/`, `modules/networking/`, and `services/*/deploy/`. You want Terraform conventions applied only when Claude edits those files. What is the best approach?","o":["Create a `.claude/rules/terraform.md` file with YAML frontmatter `paths: [\"**/*.tf\"]`.","Add a Terraform section to the root CLAUDE.md with a heading that says it applies only to .tf files.","Place a CLAUDE.md with the Terraform conventions inside each directory that contains .tf files.","Create a `/terraform` skill and ask developers to invoke it before editing infrastructure code."],"e":"Path-scoped rules load only when editing files matching the glob, regardless of directory. That saves context and avoids maintaining many directory-level files. A root section loads every session, and a skill depends on manual invocation."},{"s":2,"t":"3.2","q":"Your `/analyze-deps` skill produces thousands of lines of dependency output. After running it, the rest of the session's answers get noticeably worse. Which frontmatter change addresses this?","o":["Add `context: fork` so the skill runs in an isolated sub-agent and its output stays out of the main session.","Add `allowed-tools` listing only Read and Grep, so the skill cannot produce as much output as it does today.","Add `argument-hint` so developers pass a narrower scope each time they invoke the dependency analysis skill.","Add `disable-model-invocation: true` so the skill only runs when a developer types the command explicitly."],"e":"`context: fork` runs a skill in an isolated sub-agent context, which keeps verbose or exploratory output from polluting the main session."},{"s":2,"t":"3.2","q":"Developers often run `/scaffold-endpoint` with no arguments and get a generic result, because the skill needs a resource name and HTTP method. What is the simplest improvement?","o":["Add `argument-hint` frontmatter so the expected parameters are shown when the skill is invoked.","Add `context: fork` so the skill can ask its own follow-up questions inside an isolated context.","Move the scaffolding instructions into CLAUDE.md so they always apply to every request in the repo.","Split the skill into one skill per HTTP method so that each invocation needs fewer arguments overall."],"e":"`argument-hint` prompts developers for the parameters a skill expects, making required input explicit."},{"s":2,"t":"3.2","q":"Your team has a project `/review` skill. You want a personal version with extra checks you are experimenting with, without changing anything for teammates. What should you do?","o":["Create a personal skill in `~/.claude/skills/` with a different name, such as `/review-strict`.","Edit the project's `.claude/skills/review/SKILL.md` locally and never commit your changes.","Create `~/.claude/skills/review/` with the same name so your version overrides the team version.","Add the extra checks to `CLAUDE.local.md` so they run whenever anyone uses `/review`."],"e":"Personal variants go in `~/.claude/skills/` under a different name so they do not conflict with or replace the team's skill."},{"s":2,"t":"3.2","q":"CLAUDE.md includes 200 lines describing how to prepare release notes, a task done twice a month. It loads into every session. What is the better place for it?","o":["A skill, so the workflow loads on demand when someone is preparing release notes.","A path-scoped rule matching `CHANGELOG.md`, so it loads whenever any file is edited.","`~/.claude/CLAUDE.md`, so it only loads for the person who usually writes release notes.","A subdirectory CLAUDE.md inside the `docs/` folder where release notes are stored."],"e":"Skills are for on-demand, task-specific workflows. CLAUDE.md is for always-loaded universal standards. A rarely used multi-step workflow belongs in a skill."},{"s":2,"t":"3.4","q":"A production error has a clear stack trace pointing to a missing null check in `formatInvoiceDate()` in one file. What is the right approach?","o":["Use direct execution, since the change is small, well understood, and limited to one file.","Enter plan mode to explore the whole invoicing module and design the fix before changing any code.","Start with the Explore subagent to map every invoice-related file before making the one-line change.","Use the interview pattern so Claude can ask about invoice date requirements before it writes the fix."],"e":"Direct execution fits well-scoped changes with a known location and approach, such as a single-file bug fix with a clear stack trace."},{"s":2,"t":"3.4","q":"You must add authentication to an API. Two valid approaches (session cookies vs JWT with a token service) have different infrastructure requirements, and either will touch about 30 files. What workflow fits best?","o":["Plan mode to explore and compare the approaches, then direct execution to implement the chosen plan.","Start in direct execution with the simpler option and switch to plan mode only if unexpected problems appear.","Direct execution with detailed upfront instructions specifying exactly how each of the 30 files should change.","Implement both approaches in direct execution on separate branches, then keep whichever one passes the tests."],"e":"Multiple valid approaches, architectural impact, and multi-file changes call for plan mode. A common pattern is planning first, then direct execution to carry out the plan."},{"s":2,"t":"3.4","q":"In a multi-phase migration, the discovery phase produces huge file listings and dependency traces. By the time implementation starts, Claude has lost track of earlier decisions. What should you use?","o":["The Explore subagent, so verbose discovery runs in isolation and only a summary comes back.","Direct execution throughout, so Claude does not spend any context on planning or discovery steps.","A larger model with a bigger context window so discovery output and implementation fit together.","A `/clear` before implementation so the context window is completely empty when the coding starts."],"e":"The Explore subagent isolates verbose discovery output and returns summaries, keeping the main context focused. Clearing context throws away the findings you need."},{"s":2,"t":"3.5","q":"You described a log-line transformation in prose three times, and Claude implements it differently each time. What is the most effective next step?","o":["Give two or three concrete input/output examples of how sample log lines should be transformed.","Rewrite the prose description in much more detail, defining each term and listing every rule explicitly.","Ask Claude to restate its understanding of the requirement in its own words before writing any code.","Switch to plan mode so that Claude designs the transformation carefully before it implements anything."],"e":"When prose is interpreted inconsistently, concrete input/output examples are the most effective way to communicate the expected transformation."},{"s":2,"t":"3.5","q":"You need a caching layer for a payments service, but nobody on the team has designed cache invalidation for financial data before. What technique best surfaces important design considerations before implementation?","o":["The interview pattern: have Claude question you about failure modes and invalidation before implementing.","Concrete input/output examples showing sample cache hits and misses for the most typical payment lookups.","Direct execution with an instruction to follow industry best practices for caching sensitive payment data.","Sequential feedback: let Claude implement a first version, then fix each issue as it appears during testing."],"e":"The interview pattern has Claude ask questions that surface considerations you may not have anticipated. It is most valuable in unfamiliar domains."},{"s":2,"t":"3.5","q":"Reviewing Claude's code, you find three problems: the error-handling approach must change, and that change affects both the log format and the API response structure. How should you give the feedback?","o":["Describe all three in one detailed message, because the fixes interact and need to be designed together.","Fix them one at a time across three messages so Claude can focus fully on a single problem in each turn.","Start a new session for each of the three problems so that earlier fixes do not bias the later ones.","Fix the log format first, since it is the smallest and safest change, then decide what to do about the rest."],"e":"Interacting issues go in a single message so the fixes are designed together. Sequential iteration is better when issues are independent."},{"s":2,"t":"5.4","q":"After a long exploration session, Claude's context is full of verbose search output and its answers are getting vaguer, though you have not hit the context limit. What is the most appropriate action?","o":["Use `/compact` now to summarize the session and reduce context usage while keeping key findings.","Keep working until the context limit is reached, then let Claude Code compact automatically.","Exit and start a new session with no carried-over information so Claude starts fresh.","Ask Claude to re-read every file it explored so earlier details are back at the end of context."],"e":"`/compact` reduces context usage during extended exploration when verbose output piles up. Use it proactively for context quality, not only at the limit. Starting fresh without saving findings loses them."},{"s":3,"t":"1.3","q":"Your coordinator agent's prompt says to delegate web research to the search subagent, but every run it does the searching itself and never spawns a subagent. The AgentDefinitions are correct. What is the most likely configuration problem?","o":["The coordinator's `allowedTools` does not include \"Task\", so it has no way to spawn subagents.","The subagent descriptions are too short for the coordinator to know when each one should be used.","The coordinator needs `tool_choice` set to \"any\" so it is forced to delegate work instead of answering.","Subagents must be spawned through a PreToolUse hook rather than through the coordinator itself."],"e":"The Task tool is how a coordinator spawns subagents, and its `allowedTools` must include \"Task\". Without it, delegation is impossible no matter what the prompt says."},{"s":3,"t":"1.3","q":"The synthesis subagent responds, \"No research findings were provided,\" although the search subagent completed earlier in the coordinator's conversation. What is the cause?","o":["Subagents don't inherit the coordinator's history; the coordinator must put the findings in the subagent's prompt.","The search subagent's results expired from shared memory before the synthesis subagent was finally invoked.","The synthesis subagent's tool list is missing a read tool that gives it access to the coordinator's history.","The two subagents need a direct communication channel so search results flow into synthesis automatically."],"e":"Subagents run in isolated context with no automatic inheritance and no shared memory. Everything they need must be passed explicitly in their prompt."},{"s":3,"t":"1.3","q":"Final reports contain accurate claims but almost no citations, although the search subagent found source URLs. The coordinator passes the synthesis subagent a list of claim sentences. What is the best fix?","o":["Pass structured findings that keep each claim with its metadata: source URL, document name, and page number.","Add an instruction to the synthesis subagent's prompt requiring it to cite the original source for every claim.","Give the synthesis subagent web search access so it can find and attach the sources for each claim on its own.","Add a final citation agent that searches the web for a supporting source for each sentence in the finished report."],"e":"Attribution was lost at the handoff. Structured data that separates content from metadata preserves claim-source mappings. Telling synthesis to cite cannot recover sources it never received."},{"s":3,"t":"1.3","q":"The coordinator invokes the web search subagent, waits for it, then invokes the document analysis subagent in the next turn. The two tasks are independent. How do you reduce latency?","o":["Have the coordinator emit both Task tool calls in a single response so the subagents run in parallel.","Merge the web search and document analysis subagents into one subagent with both sets of tools.","Have the search subagent invoke the document analysis subagent directly once it finishes searching.","Cache search results so the next run can skip the web search step for similar topics."],"e":"Multiple Task calls in a single coordinator response spawn subagents in parallel. Spawning across turns serializes independent work."},{"s":3,"t":"1.2","q":"Users often ask quick factual questions (\"When was the IPCC founded?\"), but every query runs through search, analysis, synthesis, and report generation, taking several minutes. What is the best change?","o":["Have the coordinator analyze each query's requirements and invoke only the subagents it needs.","Add a cache in front of the pipeline that returns previous answers for any similar question.","Run all four subagents in parallel so the full pipeline finishes faster for every query.","Build a separate lightweight agent and have users pick which system to use before asking."],"e":"A coordinator should dynamically choose which subagents to invoke based on query complexity instead of always running the full pipeline. Parallelizing dependent stages is not possible."},{"s":3,"t":"1.2","q":"You run two search subagents to speed up research, but their results overlap heavily and the report repeats the same sources. What should the coordinator do?","o":["Have the coordinator partition the scope so each search subagent covers distinct subtopics or source types.","Deduplicate the results in the synthesis subagent and keep running both searches unchanged.","Let the two search subagents message each other to coordinate which sources each one covers.","Run only one search subagent, since parallel searching always produces overlapping results."],"e":"The coordinator should partition research scope across subagents (by subtopic or source type) to minimize duplication. Direct subagent-to-subagent communication breaks the hub-and-spoke pattern."},{"s":3,"t":"1.2","q":"A synthesized report on renewable energy policy covers solar and wind well but says almost nothing about grid storage, which the user explicitly cared about. What should the coordinator's workflow include?","o":["Run a refinement loop: check synthesis for gaps, re-delegate targeted research, and re-run synthesis until covered.","Use a stricter synthesis prompt telling it to write at least one full section on every topic the user names in the request.","Raise the search result limit for each query, so more storage-related sources are found on the very first pass.","Add a final step asking the report generation subagent to write a grid storage section from its own general knowledge."],"e":"The coordinator evaluates synthesis output for gaps, re-delegates targeted queries, and re-invokes synthesis. Writing from model knowledge without research would produce unsourced claims."},{"s":3,"t":"1.3","q":"The coordinator's prompt to the search subagent is a rigid procedure: \"Run exactly 3 queries, take the top 5 results each, summarize each in 2 sentences.\" Results are poor on niche topics. What is the better prompt design?","o":["Specify the research goal and quality criteria (coverage, credibility, recency) and let the subagent choose how.","Expand the procedure to cover more situations, such as running six queries whenever fewer than ten results are found.","Add few-shot examples of the same procedure applied to several niche topics so that the subagent copies the pattern.","Move the procedure into a PreToolUse hook so each search call is checked against the required steps before it runs."],"e":"Coordinator prompts should state goals and quality criteria rather than step-by-step procedures, so subagents can adapt to unexpected situations."},{"s":3,"t":"1.2","q":"To cut latency, a teammate proposes letting the document analysis subagent send results straight to the synthesis subagent, bypassing the coordinator. How should you evaluate this?","o":["Reject it: routing through the coordinator preserves observability, consistent error handling, and controlled flow.","Accept it: direct messaging between subagents is the recommended pattern whenever doing so measurably reduces latency.","Accept it, but only for the analysis-to-synthesis step, and keep the coordinator in place for all other communication.","Reject it, because subagents are technically unable to send messages to any agent other than the one that spawned them."],"e":"In hub-and-spoke, all subagent communication goes through the coordinator for observability, uniform error handling, and controlled routing."},{"s":3,"t":"2.3","q":"You gave the synthesis subagent the same web search tools as the search subagent \"just in case.\" Now synthesis sometimes runs its own searches and mixes unvetted sources into reports. What is the best fix?","o":["Restrict the synthesis subagent's tools to those its role needs, so it cannot perform general web searches.","Add a system prompt line telling the synthesis subagent to use search tools only as a last resort.","Add a PostToolUse hook that removes any search results the synthesis subagent retrieves itself.","Raise the search subagent's result limits so synthesis has less reason to search on its own."],"e":"Agents with tools outside their specialization tend to misuse them. Scope each subagent's tools to its role."},{"s":3,"t":"2.1","q":"The document analysis subagent has one tool, `analyze_document`, used for pulling statistics, summarizing, and checking claims. Outputs vary wildly in shape and the coordinator struggles to use them. What is the best redesign?","o":["Split it into purpose-specific tools (`extract_data_points`, `summarize_content`, `verify_claim_against_source`) with defined outputs.","Keep the single tool but add a `mode` parameter for the three jobs and describe each mode carefully in the tool's description.","Keep the tool exactly as it is and add few-shot examples of each kind of expected output to the subagent's system prompt.","Have the coordinator post-process every result with a second model call that normalizes the output into a single shape."],"e":"Splitting generic tools into purpose-specific tools with defined input/output contracts removes ambiguity and makes output predictable."},{"s":3,"t":"2.3","q":"The document analysis subagent has a general `fetch_url` tool and sometimes fetches arbitrary web pages, such as blog comments, instead of the documents it was assigned. What is the best fix?","o":["Replace `fetch_url` with a constrained `load_document` tool that validates URLs against the assigned documents.","Add a system prompt rule telling the subagent to fetch only URLs that look like formal documents rather than web pages.","Move `fetch_url` to a separate MCP server so the subagent has to request access each time before it can use the tool.","Allow the fetches to continue but add a relevance score so the synthesis agent can down-weight non-document sources."],"e":"Replacing a generic tool with a constrained alternative that validates input applies least privilege to tool design."},{"s":3,"t":"5.6","q":"Two credible sources report different figures for 2024 battery storage growth: 12% (industry association) and 8% (government agency), both using the same period. How should the synthesis present this?","o":["Include both values with their sources and note the discrepancy, so the reader can see the conflict.","Use the government figure, because government agencies are the more authoritative type of source.","Average the two figures and report 10% so the report shows a single balanced estimate.","Leave the statistic out of the report, since conflicting values cannot be considered reliable."],"e":"Conflicting statistics from credible sources should be annotated with attribution, not resolved by picking, averaging, or dropping a value."},{"s":3,"t":"5.6","q":"The synthesis agent flags a \"contradiction\": one source says EV market share is 14%, another says 18%. On inspection, they describe 2022 and 2024. What change prevents this?","o":["Require subagents to include publication or data-collection dates in their structured findings.","Instruct the synthesis agent to always prefer the most recently published source when numbers differ.","Have the search subagent return only sources from the last 12 months to avoid outdated statistics.","Ask the synthesis agent to flag contradictions only when the values differ by more than 5 points."],"e":"Dates in structured outputs let downstream agents tell temporal change from genuine contradiction. Without them, trends look like conflicts."},{"s":3,"t":"5.3","q":"The search subagent for one subtopic, offshore wind, fails after retries. The other subagents succeed. What should the final report do?","o":["Add coverage annotations showing which areas are well supported and flagging the offshore wind gap.","Omit offshore wind entirely, so the report contains only well-supported findings and reads consistently.","Fill in the offshore wind section from the synthesis model's general knowledge so the report stays complete.","Stop report generation and return an error to the user, since an incomplete report should never be delivered."],"e":"Synthesis output should carry coverage annotations so readers know which findings are well supported and where gaps exist. Silent omission hides the gap, and filling it from model knowledge adds unsourced claims."},{"s":3,"t":"5.3","q":"The search subagent hits a brief rate-limit error on one query. How should errors be handled between the subagent and the coordinator?","o":["The subagent retries transient failures itself and escalates only what it can't fix, with attempts and partial results.","The subagent passes every error straight to the coordinator so that all retry decisions are made in one central place.","The subagent quietly skips the failed query and returns the results from its other queries as a complete result set.","The subagent ends its task with a generic \"search unavailable\" status so the coordinator can decide to retry everything."],"e":"Subagents should handle transient failures locally and propagate only unresolvable errors with structured context (what was attempted, partial results). Silent skipping and generic statuses hide information from the coordinator."},{"s":3,"t":"5.1","q":"The synthesis agent receives three long subagent outputs concatenated in sequence. Its report reliably covers the first and last outputs but often misses findings from the middle one. What is the best fix?","o":["Put a key findings summary at the start of the combined input and give each output a clear section header.","Add a prompt instruction telling the synthesis agent to read every section of the combined input with equal care.","Shuffle the order of the three outputs on each run so that no single subagent is consistently placed in the middle.","Use a model with a larger context window so all three outputs fit comfortably with plenty of room to spare."],"e":"The \"lost in the middle\" effect is mitigated structurally: key findings first and explicit section headers. Prompt reminders and larger windows do not fix position effects."},{"s":3,"t":"5.6","q":"Your report on a company's sustainability progress mixes quarterly emissions figures, a news timeline, and a technical description of its carbon capture system. Everything is written as dense paragraphs. What should synthesis do?","o":["Match format to content: emissions figures as tables, news as prose, and technical findings as structured lists.","Convert everything into bullet lists, since lists are the easiest format for busy readers to scan quickly.","Convert everything into tables so that every section shares one consistent, directly comparable structure.","Keep consistent prose throughout, but bold the key numbers so readers can still find the data they need."],"e":"Synthesis should render content types appropriately (financial data as tables, news as prose, technical findings as structured lists) rather than forcing one uniform format."},{"s":4,"t":"2.5","q":"An engineer asks the agent to find every place `calculateShippingTax` is called. Which built-in tool should the agent start with?","o":["Grep, because it searches file contents for a pattern such as a function name.","Glob, because it finds files whose names match the function being searched for.","Read, opening each source file in turn and scanning it for the function name.","Bash, running `find` with the function name to match files across the repository."],"e":"Grep searches file contents (function callers, error messages, imports). Glob matches file paths by name pattern."},{"s":4,"t":"2.5","q":"The agent needs a list of every React component test file in the repo (`*.test.tsx`), spread across dozens of folders. Which tool fits best?","o":["Glob with the pattern `**/*.test.tsx`.","Grep for the word `describe(` across all files.","Read on the `src/` directory to list its contents.","Grep for `.test.tsx` within file contents."],"e":"Glob matches file paths by naming pattern, which is exactly what finding files by extension needs. Grep searches contents, not paths."},{"s":4,"t":"2.5","q":"The agent must change one `retryCount: 3` setting in a config file, but that exact block, including its surrounding lines, appears identically in four places, and only one should change. Edit keeps failing. What is the reliable fallback?","o":["Read the full file, then Write the complete updated contents with only the intended block changed.","Use Edit with `replace_all` so the change is applied consistently to all four matching blocks.","Retry Edit several times, since the tool will eventually pick the first matching occurrence.","Use Bash with `sed` to replace the first match on the assumption that it is the right one."],"e":"Edit needs a unique anchor. When the target cannot be disambiguated, Read plus Write is the reliable fallback. `replace_all` would change blocks that should stay unchanged."},{"s":4,"t":"2.5","q":"Asked to explain how refunds flow through an unfamiliar 2,000-file codebase, the agent begins reading every file in `src/` and quickly exhausts its context. What approach should it use?","o":["Read the full file, then Write the complete updated contents with only the intended block changed.","Use Edit with `replace_all` so the change is applied consistently to all four of the matching blocks.","Retry Edit several times, since the tool will eventually settle on the first matching occurrence.","Use Bash with `sed` to replace the first match, on the assumption that the first one is the right one."],"e":"Build understanding incrementally: Grep to locate entry points, then Read to follow imports and trace flows, reading only what each step justifies."},{"s":4,"t":"2.5","q":"`formatCurrency` is defined in `utils/money.ts` and re-exported from `lib/index.ts` as `fmtMoney`. A Grep for `formatCurrency` finds only two call sites, but you know it is used widely. What should the agent do?","o":["Grep for entry points such as `refund`, then Read the relevant files and follow imports as needed.","Read all files in `src/` as planned, but summarize each one immediately afterward to save context.","Glob for every file with `service` in its name and read them all before attempting to answer.","Ask the engineer to paste the files they think are relevant into the chat before starting the analysis."],"e":"To trace usage through wrapper modules, find all exported names first, then search for each across the codebase."},{"s":4,"t":"2.4","q":"Your team built an MCP tool `semantic_code_search` that understands symbols and call graphs. Its description is \"Searches code.\" The agent almost always uses built-in Grep instead. What is the most effective fix?","o":["Find every exported name for the function, including aliases like `fmtMoney`, then Grep for each.","Grep for `formatCurrency` again with case-insensitive matching to catch other spellings of the name.","Glob for files with `money` in their path and read each one to find the remaining callers by hand.","Accept the two results, since Grep searches every file in the repository and cannot miss a reference."],"e":"Sparse MCP descriptions lead agents to prefer built-in tools. Detailed descriptions of capabilities and outputs fix adoption without removing useful tools or forcing calls."},{"s":4,"t":"2.4","q":"You want every engineer to get the team's GitHub MCP server automatically when they clone the repo, without committing anyone's token. How should you configure it?","o":["Expand the MCP tool's description to explain its capabilities, inputs, outputs, and when it beats text search.","Remove Grep from the agent's available tools so that it has no choice but to use the MCP search tool instead.","Set `tool_choice` to force `semantic_code_search` on every request the agent makes, regardless of the task.","Rename the tool to `grep_plus` so the model associates it with the familiar built-in Grep tool it already prefers."],"e":"Project-scoped `.mcp.json` shares configuration through version control, and `${VAR}` expansion keeps secrets out of the repo."},{"s":4,"t":"2.4","q":"You want to try an experimental MCP server for a personal note-taking app without affecting teammates. Where should you configure it?","o":["In your user-level `~/.claude.json`, which is personal and not shared with teammates.","In the project's `.mcp.json` with a comment marking it as experimental.","In `.claude/rules/mcp.md` with path frontmatter so it loads only for you.","In `CLAUDE.local.md` beside the project CLAUDE.md."],"e":"User-scoped `~/.claude.json` is for personal or experimental servers. Project `.mcp.json` is shared with everyone."},{"s":4,"t":"2.4","q":"The team needs the agent to read and update Jira tickets using standard operations (search, comment, transition). What is the recommended approach?","o":["Use an existing community MCP server for Jira; reserve custom servers for team-specific workflows.","Build a custom MCP server for Jira so the team fully controls the behavior of every single operation.","Give the agent Bash access and have it call the Jira REST API directly with handwritten `curl` commands.","Export all Jira tickets to markdown nightly and let the agent read them with the built-in Read tool."],"e":"For standard integrations, prefer maintained community MCP servers. Build custom servers only for requirements community servers cannot meet."},{"s":4,"t":"2.4","q":"Before answering data questions, the agent repeatedly calls `list_tables` and then `describe_table` on dozens of tables just to see what exists. How can you reduce these exploratory calls?","o":["Expose the database schema as an MCP resource so the agent can see available tables and columns up front.","Combine `list_tables` and `describe_table` into one tool that returns every table's full schema.","Add the complete schema to the system prompt for every session, regardless of the question asked.","Limit the agent to five tool calls per question so it stops exploring after a few attempts."],"e":"MCP resources expose content catalogs (schemas, documentation hierarchies, issue summaries) so agents can see what data exists without exploratory tool calls."},{"s":4,"t":"1.6","q":"An engineer asks the agent to \"add comprehensive tests to our legacy billing module,\" which has no tests and unclear dependencies. Which decomposition strategy fits best?","o":["Map the module's structure, find high-impact areas, then follow a prioritized plan that adapts as you learn.","Use a fixed pipeline that generates tests for each file in alphabetical order until every file in the module is covered.","Generate tests for every function in a single pass, then delete any of the tests that fail when they are first run.","Ask the engineer for a complete list of test cases first, then implement exactly that list without any deviation."],"e":"Open-ended investigation tasks need dynamic, adaptive decomposition. Fixed pipelines suit predictable, well-defined steps."},{"s":4,"t":"1.7","q":"Yesterday you analyzed 40 files in a session named `auth-audit`. Overnight a teammate changed 3 of them; the rest are untouched. What is the most efficient way to continue?","o":["Resume with `--resume auth-audit` and tell the agent which 3 files changed so it re-analyzes only those.","Start a fresh session and have the agent re-explore all 40 files to be sure nothing is stale.","Resume with `--resume auth-audit` and continue without mentioning the changes, since it will notice.","Use `fork_session` from yesterday's session so the changed files get analyzed in a new branch."],"e":"Resume when prior context is mostly valid, and inform the agent about specific changed files for targeted re-analysis. A resumed session will not notice changes on its own."},{"s":4,"t":"1.7","q":"Since your last session, a large refactor renamed modules and changed most function signatures the agent analyzed. Resuming leads the agent to cite functions that no longer exist. What should you do?","o":["Start a new session seeded with a structured summary of the earlier findings instead of resuming.","Resume the session and ask the agent to be careful about any outdated information as it continues working.","Resume the session and run `/compact` right away so the old tool results get summarized out of context.","Fork the old session so the agent gets a clean branch to work from while keeping the same history."],"e":"When prior tool results are stale, a fresh session seeded with a structured summary is more reliable than resuming, which restores outdated tool output."},{"s":4,"t":"1.7","q":"After a thorough codebase analysis, you want to compare two refactoring strategies (extract a service vs modularize in place) without one exploration influencing the other. What should you use?","o":["`fork_session` to create two independent branches from the shared analysis.","`--resume` twice on the same session, exploring one strategy after the other.","Two brand-new sessions that each re-run the full codebase analysis from scratch.","One session that explores both strategies side by side in alternating turns."],"e":"`fork_session` creates independent branches from a shared baseline for divergent exploration. `--resume` continues one line of work."},{"s":4,"t":"5.4","q":"Two hours into exploring a legacy system, the agent starts saying things like \"this probably follows a typical repository pattern\" instead of naming the specific classes it found earlier. What is the best mitigation?","o":["Have the agent record key findings in a scratchpad file and consult it for later questions.","Move to a model with a larger context window so that the earlier findings remain in context.","Remind the agent in each new message to use the specific class names it discovered earlier.","Restart the session and let the agent rediscover the important classes from the very beginning."],"e":"Context degradation shows up as generic \"typical pattern\" answers. Scratchpad files persist findings outside the conversation. Restarting without saved state loses everything."},{"s":4,"t":"5.4","q":"A multi-agent exploration of a large monorepo occasionally crashes partway through, and all progress is lost. What design makes recovery reliable?","o":["Each agent exports state to a known location; on resume the coordinator loads a manifest into agent prompts.","Rerun the whole exploration from the start after any crash, since partial results could be inconsistent.","Keep all of the exploration within a single agent so there is only one conversation that needs resuming.","Raise the session timeout and the retry limits so that crashes happen much less often during long runs."],"e":"Structured state persistence (per-agent exports plus a coordinator-loaded manifest) lets work resume without repeating earlier exploration."},{"s":4,"t":"5.4","q":"Why spawn subagents for specific questions (for example, \"trace all callers of the refund API\") while the main agent coordinates exploration?","o":["To keep verbose exploration output in the subagent's context, so the main agent stays focused.","Mainly to run more searches in parallel, which cuts the total time the exploration takes to finish.","Because subagents share memory with the main agent and can therefore build directly on its findings.","Because the main agent loses access to Grep and Read once it has spawned at least one subagent."],"e":"Parallelism is a side benefit. The main value of subagent delegation for exploration is context isolation: verbose output stays in the subagent, and the main agent receives a summary."},{"s":5,"t":"3.6","q":"Your CI job runs `claude -p` to review PRs, and a script parses the free-text output into inline comments. Parsing breaks whenever the wording changes. What is the most reliable fix?","o":["Add `--output-format json` with `--json-schema` so findings are returned as schema-validated structured data.","Add a prompt instruction telling Claude to answer only with JSON that follows a described structure.","Write stricter regular expressions in the parser to handle more variations of the review text.","Add `--verbose` so the output includes structured debugging information the parser can use."],"e":"`--output-format json` plus `--json-schema` produces machine-parseable, schema-validated findings for posting as inline comments. Prompt-only JSON gives no guarantee."},{"s":5,"t":"4.6","q":"Your pipeline generates code with Claude and then, in the same session, asks it to review that code. The review rarely catches anything. What is the best improvement?","o":["Run the review in a separate Claude instance that has none of the generator's reasoning context.","Enable extended thinking in the generating session so that its self-review is much more thorough.","Add a prompt telling the session to be highly critical of its own code whenever it is reviewing.","Run the same-session review three times and report any issue that is found in at least one run."],"e":"A model reviewing its own output in the same session keeps its generation reasoning and tends to confirm its decisions. An independent instance without that context catches more."},{"s":5,"t":"3.6","q":"Every push to a PR re-runs the review, and developers get the same comments repeated, including ones they chose not to address. What change fixes this?","o":["Include prior findings in context and tell Claude to report only new or still-unaddressed issues.","Review only the files changed in the latest commit and ignore the rest of the pull request entirely.","Run the review only once per pull request, on the first push, and skip all of the later pushes.","Post a comment only when a finding's severity is higher than it was in the previous review run."],"e":"Including prior findings lets Claude report only new or still-unaddressed issues, avoiding duplicate comments. Reviewing only the latest commit can miss problems that span commits."},{"s":5,"t":"3.6","q":"Claude-generated tests in CI frequently duplicate scenarios already covered in the test suite. What is the most direct fix?","o":["Provide the existing test files in context so generation can avoid scenarios already covered.","Tell Claude in the prompt to make sure every generated test is different from existing tests.","Run a deduplication script after generation to remove tests with similar names.","Lower the number of tests Claude may generate per run so that fewer duplicates appear."],"e":"Claude cannot avoid duplicating tests it cannot see. Providing existing test files in context is the direct fix."},{"s":5,"t":"3.6","q":"CI-generated tests are mostly low value: trivial getters and constructors, while important edge cases go untested and existing fixtures are ignored. Where should you document what a valuable test is and which fixtures exist?","o":["In the project CLAUDE.md, which Claude Code reads in CI just as it does interactively.","In a comment block at the top of every existing test file in the repository.","In the CI job's environment variables, which Claude Code reads automatically at startup.","In each developer's `~/.claude/CLAUDE.md` so their own machines generate better tests."],"e":"CLAUDE.md is how you give project context (testing standards, valuable test criteria, fixtures) to CI-invoked Claude Code. User-level files do not exist on the CI runner."},{"s":5,"t":"4.1","q":"The review prompt says \"Check that code comments are accurate.\" Developers are flooded with nitpicks about wording and style. What prompt change best improves precision?","o":["Flag a comment only when its claimed behavior contradicts what the code actually does.","Check that code comments are accurate, and report only findings you are highly confident about.","Be conservative when reviewing comments and avoid flagging anything that might be subjective.","Check comment accuracy, but limit the review to at most three comment findings per pull request."],"e":"Explicit categorical criteria improve precision. General instructions such as \"be conservative\" or \"high confidence only\" do not reliably reduce false positives."},{"s":5,"t":"4.1","q":"Your reviewer reports many minor style issues and local-convention quibbles alongside real bugs. Adding \"only report high-confidence findings\" did not help. What should you do instead?","o":["Define what to report (bugs, security issues) and what to skip (minor style, local patterns).","Lower the temperature so the model produces fewer speculative findings on each review run.","Add \"be extremely conservative\" to the prompt alongside the existing high-confidence instruction.","Ask the model to score its confidence in each finding and filter out anything below 8 out of 10."],"e":"Specific report/skip criteria beat confidence-based filtering. Self-reported confidence is poorly calibrated."},{"s":5,"t":"4.1","q":"The \"naming conventions\" category has a very high false-positive rate, and developers have started ignoring all review comments, including accurate security findings. What is the best immediate step?","o":["Temporarily disable the naming category while improving its prompt, so trust in the others recovers.","Keep all categories enabled and add a disclaimer that naming findings may be less reliable than others.","Disable the whole automated review until every category's false-positive rate is down to an acceptable level.","Lower the severity of naming findings so that they always appear last in each review summary comment."],"e":"High false-positive categories undermine trust in accurate ones. Temporarily disable the problem category while you improve it."},{"s":5,"t":"4.1","q":"The same SQL string-concatenation issue is labeled \"critical\" on one PR and \"minor\" on another. The prompt defines severities in a sentence each. What best improves consistency?","o":["Define each severity level with explicit criteria and concrete code examples of what belongs there.","Have the model explain its reasoning before assigning a severity, without changing the definitions.","Collapse the levels into just two, \"issue\" and \"no issue,\" to remove the ambiguity between them.","Use the most severe label that any of three independent runs assigns to the same finding."],"e":"Severity criteria with concrete code examples for each level produce consistent classification. Prose definitions leave room for interpretation."},{"s":5,"t":"4.2","q":"Findings come back in inconsistent shapes: some lack a line number, others lack a suggested fix, even though the prompt lists every required part in detail. What is the most effective improvement?","o":["Add a few examples of complete findings with location, issue, severity, and fix in the exact format.","Repeat the list of required parts at both the beginning and the end of the prompt for emphasis.","Ask the model to double-check every finding for missing parts before it returns the final review.","Post-process the findings and silently drop any that are missing a line number or a suggested fix."],"e":"When detailed instructions still produce inconsistent output, few-shot examples are the most effective way to get consistent formatting."},{"s":5,"t":"4.2","q":"The reviewer keeps flagging an intentional pattern (an empty `catch` inside your retry wrapper that is designed to swallow transient errors) while still needing to catch genuinely swallowed exceptions elsewhere. What helps most?","o":["Few-shot examples contrasting the acceptable retry wrapper with a genuine swallowed-exception bug, with reasoning.","A rule telling the reviewer never to flag empty `catch` blocks anywhere in the codebase under any circumstances.","An ignore list entry for the retry wrapper file so the reviewer never sees or comments on that file again.","An instruction to flag empty `catch` blocks only when the reviewer is highly confident they are actual bugs."],"e":"Few-shot examples that distinguish acceptable patterns from genuine issues, with reasoning, reduce false positives while still catching real bugs. A blanket rule or ignore list would suppress the real ones."},{"s":5,"t":"4.4","q":"Developers dismiss many review findings, but you cannot tell which kinds of code trigger the bad ones. What should you add to the structured findings?","o":["A `detected_pattern` field naming the code construct behind each finding, so dismissals can be grouped.","A free-text field where developers explain in their own words why they dismissed each finding.","A self-reported confidence field, so that low-confidence findings can be hidden from developers by default.","A timestamp field so you can see which hours of the day produce the highest number of dismissals."],"e":"`detected_pattern` fields allow systematic analysis of which constructs produce dismissed (false positive) findings, feeding prompt refinement."},{"s":5,"t":"4.5","q":"Which of these workflows is the best fit for the Message Batches API?","o":["A weekly dependency and code-health audit whose report is read the following Monday.","A required security check that must pass before a pull request can be merged.","A bot that replies to developer questions left as comments on pull requests.","A pre-deploy gate that blocks the release pipeline until the review completes."],"e":"Batch processing (50% cost savings, up to 24 hours, no latency SLA) fits latency-tolerant work like weekly audits. Anything blocking or interactive needs the synchronous API."},{"s":5,"t":"4.5","q":"You want to batch-process nightly reviews where Claude calls a `run_linter` tool mid-review and uses its output to continue. Why won't this work as designed with the Message Batches API?","o":["Batch requests don't support multi-turn tool calling, so tools can't run mid-request and feed results back.","Batch requests don't support system prompts, so the detailed review instructions would simply be ignored.","Batch requests must each finish in under one minute, and running the linter makes reviews take too long.","Batch results arrive out of order, so the linter output cannot be matched back to the review it belongs to."],"e":"The batch API cannot execute tools mid-request and return results. Results can be correlated with `custom_id`, so ordering is not the problem."},{"s":5,"t":"4.6","q":"After switching to per-file review passes, depth is consistent, but a field renamed in `api/types.ts` broke six consumer files and no finding mentioned it. What is missing?","o":["A separate cross-file integration pass that checks data flow and contracts across files.","A larger context window for each per-file pass so it can also see all of the related files.","A rule requiring developers to list every affected file in the PR description for reviewers.","Running each per-file pass twice and merging the two resulting sets of findings together."],"e":"Multi-pass review pairs per-file local analysis with a separate integration pass for cross-file issues. Per-file passes alone cannot see cross-file breakage."},{"s":5,"t":"4.6","q":"You want to route uncertain review findings to a human reviewer instead of posting them directly. What is the sound approach?","o":["Report confidence per finding, calibrate thresholds on a labeled validation set, and route low-confidence ones to review.","Have the model report confidence with each finding and post anything above 0.8, since that is the standard cutoff.","Route every finding to human review, since model confidence scores cannot be used for routing decisions at all.","Route findings to human review based on how many lines of code changed in each file of the pull request."],"e":"Self-reported confidence can support routing once thresholds are calibrated against labeled data. Raw scores used with an arbitrary cutoff are poorly calibrated."},{"s":6,"t":"4.3","q":"Your extraction pipeline asks Claude to \"respond with JSON matching this structure.\" About 1% of responses fail to parse because of trailing commas or missing brackets. What is the most reliable fix?","o":["Define an extraction tool whose input schema is your JSON schema and read the data from the `tool_use` block.","Add stricter instructions and a complete example JSON object to the prompt showing the exact syntax required.","Use a lenient JSON parser that automatically repairs common syntax errors before validating the output.","Retry any response that fails to parse, repeating the request until a valid JSON object comes back."],"e":"Tool use with a JSON schema is the most reliable way to get schema-compliant structured output and eliminates syntax errors. Prompt-based JSON has no structural guarantee."},{"s":6,"t":"4.3","q":"You have separate extraction tools for invoices, receipts, and purchase orders, and you don't know a document's type in advance. Sometimes Claude replies with text instead of calling a tool. What `tool_choice` should you use?","o":["\"any\", so the model must call one of the tools but can choose which one fits the document.","\"auto\", so the model can decide whether a tool call is appropriate for each document.","A forced choice of `extract_invoice`, since invoices are the most common document type.","\"none\", and ask the model to state the document type before you choose a tool yourself."],"e":"`tool_choice: \"any\"` guarantees a tool call while letting the model pick the right schema. \"auto\" allows text responses, and forcing one tool picks the wrong schema for other types."},{"s":6,"t":"4.3","q":"Every document must have `extract_metadata` run first, before enrichment tools. How should you configure the first request?","o":["Set `tool_choice` to `{\"type\": \"tool\", \"name\": \"extract_metadata\"}`, then run enrichment in follow-up requests.","Set `tool_choice` to \"any\" and list `extract_metadata` first in the tools array so it is chosen first.","Set `tool_choice` to \"auto\" and tell the model in the system prompt to always call `extract_metadata` first.","Provide only the enrichment tools and call `extract_metadata` from your own code after the model responds."],"e":"Forced tool selection guarantees a specific tool runs first. Tool order in the array does not force anything, and prompt instructions are probabilistic."},{"s":6,"t":"4.3","q":"After adding a forced `extract_metadata` choice to your extraction requests, the pipeline gets stuck calling `extract_metadata` over and over and never reaches the enrichment tools. What is the most likely cause?","o":["The forced `tool_choice` is still set on follow-up requests; it applies per request and should revert to \"auto\".","The `extract_metadata` result is being sent back as plain text instead of inside a proper `tool_result` block.","The enrichment tools' descriptions are too vague, so the model keeps preferring the metadata tool over them.","Forced tool choice only behaves correctly with the Message Batches API, not with the synchronous Messages API."],"e":"`tool_choice` applies to each request. Leaving the forced choice on means every follow-up must call the same tool again. Use the forced choice for the first step, then process later steps in follow-up turns with \"auto\"."},{"s":6,"t":"4.3","q":"Many invoices have no PO number, yet every extraction includes one, often an invented value that looks plausible. `po_number` is a required string in your schema. What should you change?","o":["Make `po_number` optional or nullable so the model can return null when there is none.","Add a prompt instruction telling the model never to invent values for any field it extracts.","Add a validation step that rejects PO numbers that don't match your company's numbering format.","Lower the temperature so the model is less creative when filling in fields from the document."],"e":"Required fields push the model to produce a value even when the source lacks one. Nullable/optional fields are the primary defense against fabrication."},{"s":6,"t":"4.3","q":"Your `expense_category` enum has eight values, but new expense types keep appearing and get forced into the closest category. What schema pattern handles this best?","o":["Add an \"other\" enum value paired with a free-text detail field.","Remove the enum and let the model write any category name it chooses.","Add every expense category you can think of so the enum covers all cases.","Make the field nullable so uncategorizable expenses are simply left empty."],"e":"An \"other\" value plus a detail string keeps categories structured while capturing cases the enum does not cover."},{"s":6,"t":"4.3","q":"Some contracts are genuinely ambiguous about whether they renew automatically, and the model forces each one into \"yes\" or \"no.\" What schema change helps?","o":["Add an \"unclear\" value to the `auto_renews` enum for documents where the evidence is ambiguous.","Change the field to a confidence score from 0 to 1 instead of a categorical value.","Add few-shot examples of clear \"yes\" and \"no\" contracts so the model chooses more accurately.","Make the field required so the model has to commit to an answer for every contract."],"e":"An explicit \"unclear\" enum value lets the model represent genuine ambiguity instead of forcing a classification."},{"s":6,"t":"4.4","q":"Tool use guarantees valid JSON, but some extracted invoices have line items that do not add up to the stated total. What is the best design to catch this?","o":["Extract a `calculated_total` (sum of line items) next to the `stated_total`, then validate and retry or flag.","Switch to a stricter JSON schema with `additionalProperties: false` so mismatched totals are rejected.","Drop the line items and extract only the total, since that is the field downstream systems rely on most.","Tell the model to adjust the line items so that they always add up exactly to the stated total."],"e":"Schemas eliminate syntax errors, not semantic errors. Extracting `calculated_total` alongside `stated_total` creates a built-in discrepancy check for validation. Adjusting data to match would fabricate it."},{"s":6,"t":"4.4","q":"Pydantic validation fails on an extraction: `invoice_date` must be ISO 8601 but came back as \"March 4th.\" What should the retry request include?","o":["The original document, the failed extraction, and the specific validation error.","Only the original document, sent again with a higher temperature for a different result.","Only the validation error, with a request to output the corrected field.","The original document and a general reminder to follow the schema carefully."],"e":"Retry with error feedback sends the source, the failed output, and the specific error so the model can correct precisely."},{"s":6,"t":"4.4","q":"A field keeps failing validation because the contract says \"pricing per Schedule B (attached separately),\" and Schedule B is not in the input. Three retries have all failed. What is the right conclusion?","o":["Retries can't help because the information isn't in the source; return null and route for review.","Keep retrying with increasingly specific error messages until the model extracts a valid value.","Retry with a higher temperature so the model tries a different interpretation of the contract text.","Have the model estimate the Schedule B pricing from similar contracts it has processed before."],"e":"Retries fix format and structure errors. They cannot recover information that is absent from the provided input."},{"s":6,"t":"4.4","q":"A purchase agreement says \"payment due within 30 days\" in one section and \"net 60\" in another. Your extraction silently takes the first value. What design is better?","o":["Extract both values and set a `conflict_detected` boolean for downstream resolution.","Tell the model to always use the value from the most specific section of the document.","Tell the model to choose the longer payment term, since it is more favorable to the buyer.","Leave `payment_terms` empty whenever more than one value appears in the same document."],"e":"`conflict_detected` flags plus both extracted values preserve inconsistent source data for resolution instead of silently choosing one."},{"s":6,"t":"4.2","q":"Extracting authors from research papers works when citations are inline but returns null when the paper uses a bibliography section, even though the authors are present. What is the most effective fix?","o":["Add few-shot examples showing correct extraction from both inline citations and bibliography-style papers.","Make the `authors` field required so the model cannot return null for it.","Add a prompt instruction telling the model to look carefully for authors everywhere in the document.","Pre-process papers to delete bibliography sections so only the inline citations remain."],"e":"Few-shot examples demonstrating varied document structures fix empty or null extraction of information that is present in an unexpected format. Making the field required invites fabrication."},{"s":6,"t":"4.3","q":"Dates arrive as \"3/4/24\", \"March 4th, 2024\", and \"2024-03-04\", and downstream systems need consistency. Your schema already defines `date` as a string. What should you add?","o":["Format normalization rules in the prompt (e.g. \"all dates as ISO 8601\") alongside the schema.","Nothing more; tool use with a JSON schema already normalizes the format of string values automatically.","A separate enum field listing every date format the incoming documents might possibly use.","A rule telling the model to copy each date exactly as written, so no information is ever changed."],"e":"Schemas enforce structure, not formatting conventions. Pair strict schemas with normalization rules in the prompt."},{"s":6,"t":"4.5","q":"Your organization needs extracted results within 30 hours of document arrival. Batches can take up to 24 hours to process. How often should you submit batches to guarantee the SLA?","o":["Every 4 hours, so collection time plus worst-case processing stays within the SLA.","Every 6 hours, since 6 hours of collection plus 24 hours of processing equals exactly 30.","Once every 24 hours, since batch processing usually finishes much faster than the maximum.","Every 12 hours, with a real-time API fallback for any batch that runs longer than expected."],"e":"The 24-hour processing window is a worst case, not a typical time. A 4-hour submission window leaves margin; 6 hours leaves none for collection or operational delays."},{"s":6,"t":"4.5","q":"In a batch of 1,000 documents, 23 requests failed because the documents exceeded the context limit. What should you do?","o":["Use `custom_id` to find the 23 failures and resubmit only those, chunked into smaller pieces.","Resubmit the whole batch of 1,000 so that every result comes from the same consistent run.","Resubmit the 23 documents unchanged, since batch failures like these are usually temporary.","Switch the entire pipeline over to the synchronous API so that batch failures cannot happen."],"e":"Identify failures by `custom_id` and resubmit only those, with an appropriate modification such as chunking oversized documents."},{"s":6,"t":"4.5","q":"You are about to extract data from 80,000 scanned contracts with the Message Batches API. What step most reduces cost from resubmissions?","o":["Refine the prompt on a representative sample set first, to maximize first-pass success.","Submit all 80,000 at once, then fix the prompt based on whichever documents end up failing.","Split the 80,000 into 80 batches of 1,000 and adjust the prompt after each batch completes.","Use the synchronous API for the first 10,000 documents to warm the model up before batching."],"e":"Prompt refinement on a sample set before large batch runs maximizes first-pass success and reduces iterative resubmission costs."},{"s":6,"t":"5.5","q":"Your extraction system shows 97% overall accuracy, and management wants to remove human review for high-confidence extractions. What should you do first?","o":["Analyze accuracy by document type and field to confirm every segment performs well first.","Remove review for high-confidence extractions now, since 97% exceeds the usual industry target.","Remove review only for documents processed in the last month, when measured accuracy was highest.","Raise the confidence threshold slightly, then remove human review for everything above it."],"e":"Aggregate accuracy can hide poor performance on specific document types or fields. Validate by segment before automating."},{"s":6,"t":"5.5","q":"High-confidence extractions are now automated, and low-confidence ones go to humans. How do you detect a new error pattern that starts affecting high-confidence extractions?","o":["Stratified random sampling of high-confidence extractions for ongoing human verification.","Review only the low-confidence queue more carefully, since errors concentrate there.","Wait for downstream systems to report problems, then investigate the affected documents.","Rerun every high-confidence extraction monthly and compare the two outputs."],"e":"Only sampling the automated, high-confidence population reveals novel errors there. Stratified random sampling also measures ongoing error rates by segment."},{"s":6,"t":"5.5","q":"Your model outputs a confidence score per field. Dates at 0.9 confidence turn out to be right 95% of the time, but amounts at 0.9 are right only 80% of the time. How should you set review thresholds?","o":["Calibrate per-field thresholds on a labeled validation set and route fields below them to review.","Use a single 0.9 threshold for every field, since the model reports all confidence on the same scale.","Ignore the confidence scores and send a fixed 10% random sample of all fields to human review.","Add a prompt instruction asking the model to report its confidence for amounts more accurately."],"e":"Raw confidence is not calibrated and varies by field. Calibrate thresholds with labeled ground truth before using them for routing."},{"s":6,"t":"5.5","q":"You have two human reviewers and far more flagged extractions than they can check. How should their time be prioritized?","o":["Low-confidence fields and documents with ambiguous or contradictory source content first.","Documents in the order they arrived, so no extraction waits longer than the others.","An equal share of each document type, so reviewer time is spread evenly across the workload.","The highest-value invoices first, regardless of the model's confidence in each field."],"e":"Limited reviewer capacity should go where human judgment adds the most: low model confidence and ambiguous or contradictory sources."}];
const SCENARIOS={"1":{"name":"Customer Support Resolution Agent","short":"Customer support","desc":"You are building a customer support resolution agent using the Claude Agent SDK. The agent handles high-ambiguity requests like returns, billing disputes, and account issues. It has access to your backend systems through custom MCP tools (get_customer, lookup_order, process_refund, escalate_to_human). Your target is 80%+ first-contact resolution while knowing when to escalate."},"2":{"name":"Code Generation with Claude Code","short":"Code generation","desc":"You are using Claude Code to accelerate software development. Your team uses it for code generation, refactoring, debugging, and documentation. You need to integrate it into your development workflow with custom slash commands, CLAUDE.md configurations, and understand when to use plan mode vs direct execution."},"3":{"name":"Multi-Agent Research System","short":"Multi-agent research","desc":"You are building a multi-agent research system using the Claude Agent SDK. A coordinator agent delegates to specialized subagents: one searches the web, one analyzes documents, one synthesizes findings, and one generates reports. The system researches topics and produces comprehensive, cited reports."},"4":{"name":"Developer Productivity with Claude","short":"Developer productivity","desc":"You are building developer productivity tools using the Claude Agent SDK. The agent helps engineers explore unfamiliar codebases, understand legacy systems, generate boilerplate code, and automate repetitive tasks. It uses the built-in tools (Read, Write, Bash, Grep, Glob) and integrates with MCP servers."},"5":{"name":"Claude Code for Continuous Integration","short":"CI/CD","desc":"You are integrating Claude Code into your CI/CD pipeline. The system runs automated code reviews, generates test cases, and provides feedback on pull requests. You need to design prompts that provide actionable feedback and minimize false positives."},"6":{"name":"Structured Data Extraction","short":"Data extraction","desc":"You are building a structured data extraction system using Claude. The system extracts information from unstructured documents, validates the output using JSON schemas, and maintains high accuracy. It must handle edge cases gracefully and integrate with downstream systems."}};
